Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.00,0:00:05.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,AI agents can propose a database change in seconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.10,0:00:09.40,Caption,,0,0,0,,But during an online migration, one bad ordering can silently corrupt the result.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.40,0:00:13.80,Caption,,0,0,0,,CutoverProof puts that ordering on trial before production.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:09.00,0:00:15.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,WEBMCP 1/5   list_migration_contracts
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.00,0:00:21.30,Caption,,0,0,0,,The browser discovers five narrow WebMCP tools.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:16.00,0:00:30.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,WEBMCP 2/5   inspect_migration_contract
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.30,0:00:28.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,It lists the bounded migration contracts, then inspects the status-normalization contract:
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.10,0:00:34.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,declared phases, allowed operations, one invariant, and a hard authority boundary. No SQL has executed.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:34.00,0:00:43.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,PROMPT   check stale writes + compatibility-window ordering
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.10,0:00:39.40,Caption,,0,0,0,,I ask CutoverProof to check stale legacy writes and compatibility-window ordering before cutover.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.40,0:00:44.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,The request is plain language, but the work stays inside the declared contract.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:45.00,0:01:01.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,WEBMCP 3/5   create_change_review_draft
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.00,0:00:55.00,Caption,,0,0,0,,WebMCP creates an idempotent review draft, not an execution.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.00,0:00:59.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,The draft appears in the workspace as awaiting human review.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.70,0:01:04.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,An engineer inspects the budget, operations, invariant, and sandbox before anything runs.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:12.00,0:01:16.40,Caption,,0,0,0,,Only the visible human control starts the assessment.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:16.40,0:01:21.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,CutoverProof resets its dedicated PostgreSQL sandbox and plans bounded schedules.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.20,0:01:25.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,It executes them and checks the invariant with deterministic code.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.00,0:01:34.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,The verifier finds a real counterexample and blocks cutover.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.20,0:01:39.30,Caption,,0,0,0,,Backfill ran before a legacy update, leaving status shipped while the new lookup still says pending.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.30,0:01:43.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,The model suggested what to test; PostgreSQL decided what happened.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:48.00,0:02:02.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,WEBMCP 4/5   read_verified_migration_evidence
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.00,0:01:53.40,Caption,,0,0,0,,WebMCP reads the verifier-owned evidence: the exact failing schedule,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.40,0:01:57.80,Caption,,0,0,0,,the status-consistency invariant, and row forty-two.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.80,0:02:01.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,This is recorded from the test database, not generated by the model.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:05.00,0:02:18.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,WEBMCP 5/5   open_human_repair_review
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.00,0:02:13.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,The agent may open the repair review, but it cannot approve.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.20,0:02:19.00,Caption,,0,0,0,,A named engineer authorizes one allow-listed sandbox fix.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.00,0:02:26.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,CutoverProof replays the same failing schedule, and the invariant now passes. The claim stays precise: repair verified in sandbox.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.00,0:02:36.00,Caption,,0,0,0,,Agent prepares. PostgreSQL proves. Human authorizes.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:31.00,0:02:37.00,Tool,,0,0,0,,LIVE   cutoverproof-1021060138341.us-central1.run.app
